---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 5: CAD Leveling-Up I &mdash; Assemblies</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">Unit 2 &mdash; Manufacturing Foundations, Week 2</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 5</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">CAD Leveling-Up I: Assemblies</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Certified. Now What?</h2>
              <p>You're cleared on the machine. There's nothing to cut yet.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Before any toolpath, you need a model &mdash; and a cabinet
                isn't one part.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>What You Already Know</h2>
              <p>
                Sketches, extrudes, single-part modeling &mdash; from
                freshman year OnShape.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>What's New: Assemblies</h2>
              <p>
                A side panel, a front, a back, a control panel &mdash; each
                is its own part. An <b class="text-sn-theme">assembly</b> is
                where you relate them to each other.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Part Studio vs. Assembly</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Part Studio</b> &mdash; where you model one part (or a few related parts)</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Assembly</b> &mdash; where you insert finished parts and position them relative to each other</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Mates</h2>
              <p>
                A mate tells two parts how they relate &mdash; touching,
                aligned, at a fixed distance.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>The Mate You'll Use Most: Fastened</h2>
              <p>
                Locks two parts rigidly together, as if welded &mdash;
                exactly what a screwed-together cabinet panel joint actually
                is.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Worked Example</h2>
              <p>
                Two flat panels, each modeled separately, joined at a 90&deg;
                corner &mdash; the same joint a cabinet's side meets its
                front at.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Model real thickness, not a guess.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Put calipers on the actual sheet and model the number you
                measure &mdash; not the number printed on the label. Nominal
                12 mm plywood is rarely 12 mm. Corners won't meet
                cleanly otherwise.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">An assembly is still a design decision, not just clicking parts together.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Where two panels meet, and how, is exactly the kind of thing
                your Session 2 material choice was already anticipating.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Time</h2>
              <p>Session 6: CAD Leveling-Up II</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Building a full practice assembly, and preparing flat panels
                for CAM.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Model Two Panels</h3>
          <p>
            In a Part Studio, model two simple rectangular panels at a real
            stock thickness. <b class="text-sn-theme">Measure a piece of the
            sheet stock you'll actually cut</b> with calipers &mdash; the same
            skill from Unit 1, Session 6 &mdash; and model that number. If no
            stock is on hand, use the value your instructor gives you. Give the
            two panels different lengths so the corner joint is easy to check
            visually.
          </p>
          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Do not model at 12 mm because the label
            says 12 mm.</b> Session 6's practical is graded on exactly this,
            with a measured thickness that is deliberately not the nominal one,
            and the habit is easier to build now than to unlearn then.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Create an Assembly</h3>
          <p>
            Start a new Assembly and insert both panels into it.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Mate Them at a Corner</h3>
          <p>
            Using Fastened mates, position the two panels so they meet at a
            90&deg; corner &mdash; the way two sides of a box would.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 4: Verify the Joint</h3>
          <p>
            Zoom into the corner. Confirm there's no gap and no overlap where
            the two panels meet. If there is, check whether your modeled
            thickness actually matches on both parts.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you move on, confirm:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Both panels are modeled at a real, specific stock thickness &mdash; not a rounded guess</li>
            <li>The corner joint has no visible gap or overlap</li>
            <li>You used a Fastened mate, not just visual positioning</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>If your corner joint didn't meet cleanly on the first try, what was the actual CAD reason? If it did meet cleanly, what would you check first if it hadn't?</li>
            <li>A real cabinet has more than two panels meeting at more than one corner. Based on today, what do you expect to be harder about that?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your two-panel assembly file and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
